   258	
   259	## Design points this plan settles
   260	
   261	The specification leaves these to the builder. Each is a real fork, and
   262	each is recorded here as the answer the build will use.
   263	
   264	### D1 — Where the program reads main from
   265	
   266	The program clones `origin` into the per-digest workspace and builds the
   267	candidate there, at the declared base. It never uses the caller's worktree
   268	as the source of unchanged content, and it reads the caller's worktree
   269	exactly once, for the declared paths (specification field 1).
   270	
   271	Tests need this to point somewhere other than GitHub. Per B3a, pushing
   272	tests target a throwaway local bare repository, fresh per test. The program
   273	therefore takes the remote from an explicit argument that defaults to the
   274	invoking repository's `origin`, so a test can hand it a fixture path
   275	without any special test-only code path in the program.
   276	
   277	### D2 — Options not yet built
   278	
   279	Slice 1 does not implement `--no-wait`, `--import` with a value, or the
   280	`status`, `cancel`, and `imports` commands. Reaching one of them must not
   281	be an unnamed ending, and it must not exit 2 (B1 reserves exit 2 for
   282	program defects, so an argument-parser rejection would make a loop counter
   283	read a scoping decision as a gatekeeper bug).
   284	
   285	New catalog entry, `unbuilt-option`: a normal three-part refusal, exit 1,
   286	naming the option, the slice that builds it, and the available alternative
   287	("resubmit with `--wait`"). It is removed entry by entry as each slice
   288	lands, and is gone after slice 5.
   289	
   290	### D3 — The value of the `Gatekeeper-agent` trailer
   291	
   292	B6 requires `Gatekeeper-agent: <runtime/model>` on every commit, never
   293	omitted. The environment does not carry it: this box exposes
   294	`CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` and `AI_AGENT=claude-code_2-1-220_agent`, which
   295	name the runtime and its version but not the model, and the model is the
   296	half the fix ladder needs.
   297	
   298	The program therefore takes it as a required declared field, resolved once
   299	at screening like every other environment-derived value. The caller is the
   300	only party that knows which model it is. This is the cooperative class — a
   301	caller can declare wrongly — consistent with the design's honest-singleton
   302	stance: the gatekeeper records what it is told and never guesses.
   303	
   304	### D4 — A moved main, before slice 3
   305	
   306	Slice 1 pushes once. If the push is rejected because main moved since the
   307	declared base, the reply is a refusal named `main-moved`, carrying the
   308	files, the intervening commits, and the next action: update from main,
   309	rebase the work, resubmit. Nothing is left behind; the workspace is swept.
   310	
   311	`main-moved` retires in slice 3, where the same condition splits into
   312	automatic integration (clean re-application, the usual case) and `conflict`
   313	(the new main touched the same content).
   314	
   315	## Open — awaiting the user
   316	
   317	1. **The push credential.** Slice 1 ships fully tested against local bare
   318	   repositories, but it cannot check anything into
   319	   `nedschorus/nedschorus` from this box: the box is authenticated as
   320	   `ubuntu-claude`, and branch protection restricts pushes to `NedLern`.
   321	   The design's whole premise is that the program holds the project's one
   322	   push-capable credential. So slice 1 lands as a working, dormant gate,
   323	   and a separate decision — install the `NedLern` credential for the
   324	   program's use on this box, or run the program on the Mac, or move
   325	   straight to the dedicated-identity rung the design names — turns it
   326	   live. Not choirmaster's decision to make.
   327	2. **The `write-test-plan` skill.** Founding plan open question 8 names
   328	   [nedschorus#18](https://github.com/nedschorus/nedschorus/issues/18) as
   329	   the first expected candidate-skill pull, triggered by exactly this task.
   330	   Building it first would produce this slice's test plan; not building it
   331	   means the test plan above is hand-written, as it currently is.
   332	
   333	## How this plan was ruled
   334	
   335	Walked with the user 2026-08-08 and closed at the first item by his direction:
   336	he is not picky about the slice boundaries, he wants progress, and he wants
   337	more than the first slice delivered. So the decomposition, the slice-1
   338	boundary, the classification table, and the four design points D1-D4 stand as
   339	written above — accepted by not being contested — and the build proceeds
   340	through the slices in order without per-item approval.
   341	
   342	Two consequences recorded here because they change what gets built:
   343	
   344	- The `write-test-plan` skill (open item 2) is NOT built first. Founding plan
   345	  open question 8 anticipated pulling it at this task, but building a skill
