docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:55:3. **The base** — the exact commit id of the main state the work started from. Computed by the program, not declared (ruled 2026-08-10, replacing a caller-supplied `--base` field and its two hand-off refusals): the program runs `git merge-base HEAD origin/main`, after a fetch, in the caller's checkout — the same checkout it already reads declared content from — so every caller gets the exact right value deterministically, with no relay step to garble and no dependence on the cooperative tier being present. A failed fetch is tolerated: the computation proceeds against the last-known origin/main, degrading to a staler base, which the behind-main integration mechanism absorbs. The result is by construction a real commit on main. Accepted residual: a caller who refreshed from main mid-task (e.g. `git pull` after starting the work) presents a too-new fork point, weakening conflict detection for that request — a blind spot the previous design shared exactly, since its wrapper derived the value from the same repo state.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:60:8. **The digest** — SHA-256 over: base id + sorted path list + each path's new bytes (deletions as a marker) + the import triple. Deliberately excluded: message, issue, mode, origin, agent, time — the digest identifies **the work**, so identical work resubmitted under different metadata still deduplicates. Canonical serialization (the built program's): each component length-prefixed under a NUL-framed field tag, so no crafted content can make two different requests read as one (length prefixes added 2026-08-12 — the original tag-only framing was collidable, Codex finding G25). Computed by the program; callers generate nothing. Scope, ruled 2026-08-10: this deduplication recognizes verbatim resubmits only — the same work against the same base. A request rebuilt after a refresh from main digests fresh; when that work is already checked in, its declared paths now match main (unless main has since moved them) and the answer is `unchanged-path`. Accepted residual: the situations that reach that answer are rare (a successor session with an imperfect handoff, duplicate work), and the refusal's text already names the operative fact.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:78:2. **Instant screening — synchronous in both modes.** Form validation (above). The digest is computed and looked up in history right here: already present answers `already-checked-in <commit>` with no work done. A non-waiting caller gets `accepted <digest>` now; everything after runs identically either way. ("Instant" means synchronous and seconds-fast, not I/O-free: the built program creates the workspace clone during screening.)
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:79:3. **Build the candidate.** In the program's own private workspace (`<workspace-root>/<digest>/`) — never the agent's worktree — start from main *at the computed base* and apply exactly the declared changes. Unchanged files come from main, never from the agent's possibly-stale copies. A declared import happens here: copy from the legacy repository at the declared legacy commit; record the source for the trailer.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:146:- **The layout amendment (ruled 2026-08-09, C1/C3 — applying it needs an org owner):** the pusher role moves to a **dedicated GitHub account** — a collaborator with write on this one repository, not admin, never an org owner — and protection's push restriction names it alone (the account's name is recorded here verbatim when the amendment is applied). Chosen over the App/CI form this section previously sketched: an App or CI job relocates the gate into CI, which this design scopes out; a plain account keeps the gate a local program. Blast radius of a stolen token: commits to this one repository, nothing else. Owner power stays with the user; "no agent ever holds it" is the C3 target state, true at amendment application — until then `NedLern` is both pusher and owner, the live-state exception recorded above.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:147:- **The Unix-user boundary (C2) — where the enforcement actually lives:** GitHub restricts accounts, not processes, and on one machine every process of one Unix user reads the same credential files. So the main-capable credential is owned by a **dedicated Unix user**, unreadable by agent sessions, and agents invoke the program through a sudoers rule scoped to exactly it. That is the step at which "agents never push" becomes impossible rather than instructed; it also makes the gatekeeper literally an invocable service any agent on the box can call. The sudoers rule names a **root-owned copy of the program outside every checkout** (ruled 2026-08-10) — a rule pointing into a checkout would privilege whatever bytes an agent last wrote there. The copy keeps itself current from main automatically (no manual deploy step; a checked-in fix is live on the next call), which is safe because the gatekeeper's source joins the instruction-file class: changes reach main only with walked-approval evidence, enforced by the review-evidence check (slice 6). Activating the privileged lane therefore waits on slice 6. Working path: `/usr/local/lib/nedschorus-gatekeeper/git-gatekeeper.py`. Staleness of the deployed copy is fail-safe — a stale copy enforces the old contract and never runs bytes an agent planted in place directly — only walked-approved content from main; ownership, not freshness, carries the security guarantee, so a lag in self-update costs availability, never safety. Standing principle, project-wide: a deployed copy of anything keeps itself current from its source automatically rather than relying on a remembered deploy step.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:151:- **The test seams need no privileged-mode guard (C7 struck to zero, user-ruled 2026-08-10):** `--repo` and `--remote` exist so tests can hand the program throwaway repositories, and they behave identically under every user. The formerly planned refusals guarded nothing — a foreign GitHub remote dies at authentication because the credential is scoped to this one repository (C1), a non-GitHub remote (a local path) needs no credential and was never the protected asset, and refusing `--repo` would only move caller control to the working directory, not remove it. A hard-coded remote pin was considered as defense-in-depth against future credential misconfiguration and declined: the token's scope, set once by the user, is the guard.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:159:| Review-evidence field + check | No artifact class was gated at founding; the first class was designated 2026-08-04 (instruction-bearing text, nedschorus#31) | Slice 6 — waits on the walked-approval evidence format; scheduled 2026-08-10 as a prerequisite of privileged-lane activation |
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:174:The legacy system's `git-clean-slate-plan.md` (read-only reference, `~/Projects/nedlern/docs/working/proposed/`) designed the many-writer version of this problem. This section is the one classification record for the rewrite policy (founding plan § Standing decisions), in its four-class vocabulary. This design keeps only two of its ideas, re-derived (**update-feature**): workflow rules expressed as CLAUDE.md documentation, and protection-as-lock reduced to one credential. Never imported (**remove-feature** — multi-writer machinery; this project has one writer): the three GitHub Apps, the credential helper, per-agent branches, the PR pipeline for ordinary work, the parking states. The repo's git config is minimal and stated here, not imported (**preserve-feature**, contract kept and values re-derived): `user.name`/`user.email` for the machine identity, `useConfigOnly` so no global identity leaks in. No consider-feature entries, so nothing goes to `legacy-feature-queue/`.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:180:T1 every form error refuses with its named error and no side effects · T2 happy path: the four success guarantees verified, trailer exact · T3 digest: identical resubmit answers `already-checked-in`; changed content digests fresh; metadata-only changes do not change the digest · T4 concurrent submissions (injected delay): winner clean, loser integrates over newer commits and succeeds · T5 conflict: same-path collision refuses with files + commits + next action · T6 retry cap: sustained head movement ends in `main-moving-too-fast`, never a spin · T7 crash recovery: worker killed mid-WORKING → `status` reports abandoned, resubmit sweeps and completes; killed after push → resubmit answers `already-checked-in` · T8 cancel: all four branches (two return `cancelled`); cancel-after-push answers `too-late` · T9 advisory: undeclared worktree changes noted, never blocking · T10 retired (2026-08-10): its subject, the `imports` subcommand, was deleted — the trailer's exactness is covered by T2's trailer assertion and T11's screening · T11 each import error class refuses correctly · T12 retired (2026-08-10): its subject, the trailer-absence audit, was deleted from the design; slice 5's audit acceptance is B3c's three branch-protection-audit outcomes.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:184:- **Cross-machine callers (C8):** the gatekeeper reads declared content from the caller's worktree, which requires a shared filesystem; Mac-side agents cannot be read from the Ubuntu box. Candidate shapes, none chosen: the caller pushes its branch and the request names a ref instead of relying on worktree bytes (a real contract change); Mac agents route work through the Ubuntu side; or a second credentialed host (weakens the single-place property of C2). Decide when a Mac-side agent first needs direct check-in.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:185:- The walked-approval evidence format (what walked approval looks like as a checkable artifact) — undesigned; slice 6 waits on it.
docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md:189:- Which artifact classes are gated on walked-approval evidence — the first class was designated (boss-ruled 2026-08-04): instruction-bearing text (CLAUDE.md files, skills with their prompt templates, injected system prompts, the wiki), whose check-ins require walked-approval evidence; class definition, procedure, and guards on [nedschorus#31](https://github.com/nedschorus/nedschorus/issues/31). The check itself is slice 6, scheduled 2026-08-10 as a prerequisite of privileged-lane activation.

